  frame:
    shape: L-shape (2 sides) or U-shape (3 sides) of a 3-7 x 4-6 bbox, 1-cell thick
    sides: 2-3 adjacent orthogonal sides of the bbox; open side(s) face a grid edge
    position: open side(s) flush with grid edge; solid rect + frame assembly occupies a corner/edge of the grid
    gap: exactly 1 empty row/col between solid rect and each present frame wall (so bbox gap = 2 counting the wall)
  rule: |
    From each outer corner of the solid rect, trace the outward diagonal. If the
    first non-bg pixel on that ray is a corner cell of the frame (has frame neighbors
    in both an orthogonal row and column), the ray is "active": paint every bg cell
    beyond that frame corner along the same diagonal to the grid edge with the solid rect's color.
  active_rays: 1-2 per example; L-frames produce 1, U-frames produce 2
  trail_length: 1-5 pixels per active ray; >=1 required (assert in solver)

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first (place frame+solid assembly, then verify trail space)
  steps:
    1. Pick grid size N x N (6-12), two distinct non-bg colors for solid and frame.
    2. Choose frame shape (L or U) and orientation (which corner of grid).
    3. Choose frame bbox dimensions (h 3-7, w 4-6) and solid rect dimensions fitting inside with gap=1 on each frame-wall side.
    4. Place the assembly at the chosen grid corner, open side(s) flush with grid edge.
    5. Verify each active corner's outward diagonal has >=1 bg cell beyond the frame corner before hitting the grid edge. If not, increase grid size or adjust.
    6. Input = grid with frame + solid rect. Output = input + painted diagonal trail pixels.

